  "content": {
    "title": "Denver comes back to stun Wisconsin in NCAA men's hockey championship",
    "text": "Updated April 11, 2026, 8:13 p.m. CTLAS VEGAS – Wisconsin’s dream of a national championship slipped away in the third period.The Badgers men's hockey team held Denver scoreless for the first two periods but allowed two goals during the final 12 ½ minutes and suffered a 2-1 loss at T-Mobile Arena on Saturday, April 11.Senior Rieger Lorenz scored the game-tying goal at the 7:31 mark of the period and Kyle Chyzowski scored the game-winner at the 14:08 mark.Wisconsin (24-13-2) lost despite owning a 30-15 edge in shots on goal.The Badgers took a 1-0 lead at the 13:36 mark of the first period, but couldn’t add to that advantage.The loss ended Wisconsin’s deepest NCAA Tournament run in 16 years. Wisconsin was chasing its seventh national championship.Denver finished 29-11-3 and won its third title in five seasons.Check jsonline later for more on the game.Check for updates on the UW-Denver NCAA final throughout the day.Wisconsin vs Denver box score Wisconsin vs Denver box scoreTeam1 Period2 Period3 PeriodFinalDenver0022Wisconsin1001 A look Denver's go-ahead score Denver strikes again, Badgers trail, 2-1 with 6 minutes to playIf a seventh national championship is in the cards for Wisconsin, it is going to have to come from behind to make it happen.The Badgers allowed their second goal of the third period at the 14:08 mark. Kyle Chyzowski got the score and Boston Buckberger and Garrett Brown got the assists.The goal came during a extended possession for the Pioneers that had to feel even longer for UW after goaltender Daniel Hauser lost his stick in an attempt make a save earlier in the possession.Denver 2, Wisconsin 1 at the 14:08 mark of the third period Denver evens the score with 12 1/2 minutes to playWisconsin holds a 24-6 edge in shots on goal, but on the scoreboard it’s 1-1.Denver’s Rieger Lorenz scored at the 7:31 mark of the third period. Wisconsin’s Daniel Hauser made the initial save on the play but Lorenz was able to put away the rebound.Garrett Brown and Kristian Epperson, a Mequon native, got the assists.Wisconsin 1, Denver 1, 12:29 left in third period Tale of the tape after two periodsScore: Wisconsin 1, Denver 0Shots: Wisconsin 21, Denver 5Saves: Wisconsin 5, Denver 20Faceoffs: Wisconsin 20, Denver 22Power plays: Wisconsin 0-1; Denver 0-1. Wisconsin limiting chances for Denver's top lineWe’re halfway through the game and Denver’s No. 1 line of center Samu Salminen and wings James Reeder and Rieger Lorenz have combined for one shot.The game has been tough for the Pioneers in that regard. They had just four shots on goal during the first 30 minutes.That said, it’s still just a one-goal game.Wisconsin 1, Denver 0, 9 minutes left in second period. Another look at Vasily Zelenov's first peroid goalWith Vasil Zelenov's goal, the Badgers have scored first in each NCAA Tournament game. Tale of the tape after first periodScore: Wisconsin 1, Denver 0Shots on goal: Wisconsin 10, Denver 2Saves: Wisconsin 2, Denver 9Faceoffs: Wisconsin 9, Denver 11Power plays: Wisconsin 0-1; Denver 0-1 Vasily Zelenov gets UW on the boardOne minute after failing to cash in on a power play, the Badgers scored an even strength goal off the stick of freshman Vasily Zelenov at the 13:36 mark of the first period.Freshman Weston Knox got the primary assist on the play an outlet pass that gave Zelenov all of the neutral zone to build up steam. He fired the shot just before he reached the left circle.UW owned an 8-2 edge in shots on goal at that point.Wisconsin 1, Denver 0 with 8:24 left in first period. Badgers kill early penaltyWisconsin’s Oliver Tulk (tripping) was called for the first penalty of the afternoon, but the Badgers penalty kill didn’t allow a shot on goal during the 2 minutes.Wisconsin 0, Denver 0, 14:10 left in first period. The lines for the Badgers and PioneersWisconsin-Denver tale of the tapeScoring offense: Wisconsin 3.71 (fifth nationally); Denver 3.62 (8).Scoring defense: Wisconsin 2.97 (32); Denver 2.12 (4)Scoring margin: Wisconsin .74 (14); Denver 1.50 (I5)Power-play percentage: .265 (6), Denver .188 (37)Penalty-kill percentage: .722 (58); Denver .818 (23)Coaching legend offers Mike Hastings adviceAfter the Badgers defeated North Dakota in the semifinals Thursday, Badgers coach Mike Hastings received a text message from former Boston College coach Jerry York.“He just said enjoy tomorrow's practice,” Hastings said. “We always thought it was the best practice of the year and then he put dot, dot, dot, but it was hard to schedule that one.”Getting to the final possible practice of the season means reaching the national championship game. York coached for 50 seasons. He spent the final 28 at Boston College where he led the Eagles to eight NCAA finals and four championships.As Hastings planned the team’s final practice of the season, he kept York’s message in mind.“To me it's about us having energy tomorrow because we're going to need a lot of it,” Hastings said. “And so we went out for a short period of time. (The players) had a lot to do with what we were doing with practice.“I went to the leadership group and said, ‘Hey, here's some ideas I've got. What are you guys feeling?’ And so they ended up putting into that. It was a good 25-minute scheme.”",
